WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.840
Rosa, a self-employed electrician, applies for a medical plan.

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:07.680
Her completed application and first premium are the offer.

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:11.947
The insurer accepts by issuing the policy as applied for.

00:00:11.947 --> 00:00:15.787
If the insurer issues it with changes, that is

00:00:15.787 --> 00:00:19.627
a counteroffer she accepts by paying or keeping it.

00:00:19.627 --> 00:00:23.893
Consideration flows both ways: Rosa gives premium and truthful statements;

00:00:23.893 --> 00:00:27.733
the insurer gives its promise to pay covered expenses.

00:00:27.733 --> 00:00:32.000
Both parties must be competent, meaning of legal age and

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:36.267
sound mind, and the contract must have a legal purpose.

00:00:36.267 --> 00:00:39.680
A contract missing any element is not enforceable.

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:41.886
Rosa did not negotiate the wording.

00:00:41.886 --> 00:00:46.299
The insurer wrote the policy and she took it or left it.

00:00:46.299 --> 00:00:49.608
That makes it a contract of adhesion, and courts

00:00:49.608 --> 00:00:52.550
read genuine ambiguities against the drafter, the insurer.

00:00:52.550 --> 00:00:57.698
The contract is also aleatory: the values exchanged are unequal and depend on chance.

00:00:57.698 --> 00:01:01.007
Rosa might pay small premiums and collect a large

00:01:01.007 --> 00:01:04.316
hospital bill, or pay for years and collect nothing.

00:01:04.316 --> 00:01:09.097
Neither party is cheated by that imbalance, because both agreed to accept it.

00:01:09.097 --> 00:01:12.038
These two characteristics explain why insurance disputes are

00:01:12.038 --> 00:01:14.980
decided differently from disputes over an ordinary purchase.

00:01:14.980 --> 00:01:16.710
The policy is personal.

00:01:16.710 --> 00:01:21.035
It covers Rosa, and she cannot hand it to a

00:01:21.035 --> 00:01:25.359
neighbor; the insurer agreed to insure her health, not anyone’s.

00:01:25.359 --> 00:01:26.657
It is unilateral.

00:01:26.657 --> 00:01:29.684
Only the insurer makes an enforceable promise.

00:01:29.684 --> 00:01:33.144
Rosa is not legally required to keep paying,

00:01:33.144 --> 00:01:36.171
but if she stops, the promise lapses.

00:01:36.171 --> 00:01:37.469
It is conditional.

00:01:37.469 --> 00:01:42.226
The insurer’s duty to pay depends on conditions being met, such

00:01:42.226 --> 00:01:46.551
as premiums paid and proof of loss submitted on time.

00:01:46.551 --> 00:01:50.875
When a question asks why an insured cannot be sued

00:01:50.875 --> 00:01:55.200
for nonpayment, the answer is that the contract is unilateral.

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:59.344
Because the insurer drafted the contract, ambiguous language is

00:01:59.344 --> 00:02:03.488
interpreted the way a reasonable insured would understand it.

00:02:03.488 --> 00:02:09.474
The reasonable expectations doctrine goes a step further in some courts: coverage the

00:02:09.474 --> 00:02:14.999
buyer reasonably expected may be honored even if fine print says otherwise.

00:02:14.999 --> 00:02:17.762
Two more principles shape health insurance.

00:02:17.762 --> 00:02:22.827
Indemnity means the policy restores a loss but should not create

00:02:22.827 --> 00:02:27.431
profit, which is why medical expense plans reimburse actual charges.

00:02:27.431 --> 00:02:30.194
The last principle is good faith.

00:02:30.194 --> 00:02:34.798
Both sides owe each other the highest degree of honesty.

00:02:34.798 --> 00:02:40.784
The exam calls that duty “utmost good faith.” Rosa relies on the insurer

00:02:40.784 --> 00:02:46.770
to keep its promises, and the insurer relies on the answers she gives.

00:02:46.770 --> 00:02:50.818
Rosa’s application answers are representations: statements she believes true.

00:02:50.818 --> 00:02:55.316
A material misrepresentation, one that would have changed the underwriting

00:02:55.316 --> 00:02:59.364
decision, lets the insurer rescind within the contestable period.

00:02:59.364 --> 00:03:05.211
A warranty is a statement guaranteed to be true, and in health insurance

00:03:05.211 --> 00:03:10.609
statements are treated as representations, not warranties, unless the policy says otherwise.

00:03:10.609 --> 00:03:14.657
Concealment is deliberately withholding a material fact the insurer

00:03:14.657 --> 00:03:18.705
did not ask about but should have been told.

00:03:18.705 --> 00:03:22.303
Fraud is intentional deception to gain a benefit.

00:03:22.303 --> 00:03:25.901
The difference is intent and materiality, and the

00:03:25.901 --> 00:03:29.050
remedies grow more severe along that line.

00:03:29.050 --> 00:03:33.263
Suppose Rosa pays her premium twelve days late and the

00:03:33.263 --> 00:03:37.055
insurer accepts it without comment, then does so again.

00:03:37.055 --> 00:03:41.268
By voluntarily giving up its right to insist on prompt

00:03:41.268 --> 00:03:45.481
payment, the insurer has waived that right for those payments.

00:03:45.481 --> 00:03:50.958
Estoppel is the consequence: having led Rosa to rely on its conduct, the

00:03:50.958 --> 00:03:56.435
insurer is stopped from later denying a claim because a payment was late.

00:03:56.435 --> 00:04:01.070
Waiver is the voluntary surrender of a known right; estoppel prevents

00:04:01.070 --> 00:04:05.283
a party from reversing course after another relied on it.

00:04:05.283 --> 00:04:10.760
Together they keep insurers from using technicalities after their own conduct excused them.
